{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792764338,"nonce":"C+BCxiFIYm4VfUh1F+EzbkihD1s=","message":"2026-10-23 startup architecture audit at 75069892e50b64a7f962a99ac6c9d002aefc138a (compiler build 6ec332ee6bf2f380, current release binary and matching embedded snapshot).\n\nMethod: 135 sequential fresh-process invocations across all 12 CI kernels: 3 repetitions of default, forced interpreter-first, and explicit eager LLVM; one entry-MIR-cache hit per kernel; one empty-cache no-snapshot run per kernel; no-cache runs for three representative kernels. Temporary source copies add a trace marker at main entry; benchmark work is otherwise unchanged, Fibonacci pure reuse is disabled, and original sources are untouched. Successful default and LLVM outputs match for each kernel. Cold means an empty project cache, not a flushed OS page cache. Other compiler/conformance workloads were using this machine, so wall-time ranges are wide; the raw results retain them and these are diagnostic measurements, not pinned-machine performance guarantees.\n\nDefault source-to-first-main median / one warm-entry-cache first-main observation, ms:\nnbody 108 / 25; fibonacci 73 / 30; deltablue 295 / 37; mandelbrot 230 / 54; binarytrees 227 / 35; fannkuchredux 217 / 34; spectralnorm 198 / 27; fasta 244 / 41; knucleotide 210 / 44; revcomp 237 / 29; pidigits 349 / 32; regexredux 243 / 30.\n\nMeasured and source-confirmed startup costs:\n1. resolve_tier_config overrides start_interpreted=false on every CLI preset unless an explicit manifest [tier] exists. This bypasses the fast-interpreter-start branch and applies whole-module O2 before main. Default optimizer medians span 7-165 ms; DeltaBlue's 165 ms and pidigits' 156 ms dominate their startup. Expensive passes include repeated InsertFree, unswitching/unrolling, inlining and LICM. Dominator/loop analyses are recomputed by multiple passes, and PassManager repeats full pipelines. Cache analyses with explicit invalidation, schedule required free insertion after transformations that invalidate it, and move optional O1/O2 work to actual hot regions rather than deleting it permanently.\n2. Initial native compilation is whole-module and uses Cranelift speed despite Baseline's declared none policy. initialize_loaded_modules synchronously compiles the retained module before executing startup hooks. Fix the fast baseline ABI/correctness pipeline, then compile main and the required initializer call closure with stable lazy-call/vtable/reflection thunks. Higher tiers should compile only the requested root/closure; Standard/Optimized Beadie currently constructs a fresh backend over every module again.\n3. Tiering does not recover the performance sacrificed by removing upfront optimization. All default runs report one profiled entry call, all compiled functions at Baseline and zero Beadie routes/installs. Native direct calls bypass record_call and run_file disables OSR probes. Implement native call/backedge hotness and safe continuation/OSR, then enable asynchronous region optimization. Merely setting start_interpreted=true or choosing O0 is not an adequate implementation.\n4. A snapshot cache hit still reads/parses the import graph before checking artifacts; the hit path parses each source again for StaticSigIndex. Sys-heavy kernels restore about 54 imports and materialize about 1944-2023 MIR functions, then prune 92-96% (e.g. binarytrees 1961-\u003e84, fannkuch 1944-\u003e83, regexredux 2023-\u003e137). Import preparation medians are 69-113 ms on these kernels, including 14-31 ms discovery and 30-49 ms artifact restore. Persist dependency/header/signature data in BLADE so restore can bypass source reparsing; load symbols and MIR bodies on demand and determine reachability before decoding/copying/renumbering full bodies. BLADE and bsym already use Postcard binary; eagerness is the main problem, not JSON decoding.\n5. Stdlib symbol loading eagerly restores the complete manifest in two passes for every cold unit; measured median stdlib setup is 17-30 ms even for tiny programs. Replace it with an indexed prelude and module/type declaration records restored when requested. Preserve source/config validation, especially for stock Haxe stdlib and changed defines. The embedded MIR snapshot helps Sys-heavy cases substantially (source frontend without it reaches roughly 300-350 ms), but the full symbol-manifest setup remains a floor for tiny programs.\n6. Unreachable source bodies still go through AST/TAST/HIR/MIR before tree shaking. A separate synthetic program whose only work is one trace has median process CPU 45 ms with 0 unused methods, 85 ms with 1000, and 336 ms with 5000. Wall medians are 47, 89, and 351 ms. All unused methods are discarded afterward. Separate declaration/type validation from demand-driven body MIR/codegen and persist validated typed bodies so fresh processes do not repeat this work.\n7. MIR is cloned for import renumbering, stdlib assembly, the entry result and tree-shake input; runtime mappings are constructed/combined repeatedly. Each startup builds a baseline backend and three Beadie adapters, and the first whole-module compile creates another baseline backend. These are confirmed allocation/copy paths, not all individually timed. Prefer shared immutable per-module IR, sparse remapping, build-time immutable runtime tables with plugin overlays and lazy tier-adapter construction.\n8. Entry cache validation recomputes a transitive source hash and scans class-path mtimes before cache lookup. Entry artifacts are serialized and synchronously written before execution; cache files are read even before comparing their header hash. Use persisted dependency fingerprints with content hashing on changed inputs, indexed metadata reads, deferred atomic writes and a CPU/ABI-qualified native object cache. The latter addresses the remaining warm-process JIT cost; it does not replace fresh source startup work. Global BUILD_ID invalidation also includes unrelated test/backend source changes and should be split only with an explicit sound artifact ABI/semantic version policy.\n9. The linked executable loads libLLVM and Apple framework dependencies even when no function will reach LLVM. Lazy LLVM loading and smaller runtime/feature dependencies remain valid platform-specific startup levers. Prior NUC loader profiles in this issue quantify the LLVM cost; this run confirms the dependency with otool but does not claim a new isolated dyld timing (DYLD statistics produced no data).\n10. Macro expansion, haxelib/package resolution, native plugin/HDLL loading and resource reads are additional project-dependent cold paths. The 12 kernels do not exercise heavy build/init macros. They require per-file/dependency traces and validated expansion/header caches keyed by macros, defines, assets and library identities; no macro speedup is inferred from these kernel runs.\n\nCorrectness blockers were verified on the unmodified sources too: interpreter-first deltablue SIGSEGV, fannkuchredux invalid Ptr(Void) return and SIGABRT, revcomp/regexredux String-to-i64 interpreter errors. A minimal side-effect fixture prints SIDE_EFFECT twice after bailout; execute_function restarts its original call instead of resuming the frame. In an additional O0/O1 sweep, 11 kernels completed at both levels with matching emitted outputs, while fannkuchredux failed at both; several completing kernels incurred large post-main slowdowns. Fix these before changing the default tier policy.\n\nObservability gap: the CLI jit timer measures only compile_module registration and reports microseconds. It excludes backend construction, native compilation inside initialize_loaded_modules, startup hooks, and explicit LLVM promotion. Measure process-to-main, first useful output, complete frontend stages, native setup/finalization, static init and in-execution compilation separately. The benchmark_startup host also forces eager baseline plus O2 and is not a measurement of interpreter-first CLI policy.\n\nComparison: a fresh-output hxcpp DeltaBlue build took 9.347 s on this machine; five fresh launches of its resulting executable had a 14.366 ms median, with a 297.733 ms first-launch outlier. These differ from source-to-main and are not conflated with Rayzor's warm in-process benchmark throughput. The current 12-kernel audit does not reproduce hxcpp-build-scale source delays, but the frontend/eager-optimization/native-launch gap remains substantial.\n\nImplementation order: (1) trustworthy end-to-end timing and the minimal-tier correctness/resume blockers; (2) honor tier presets, use a correct cheap baseline and native hotness with asynchronous optimization; (3) selective BLADE declaration/MIR restore and cached import/signature graphs; (4) demand-driven typed-body lowering and per-function native compilation; (5) native object cache, deferred atomic writes, lazy LLVM/brokers, and allocation/table cleanup. Preserve user initializer effects exactly once and retain a full-validation mode.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/rayzor-startup-audit/summary.csv and summary.json; results.json and per-process events; scaling-repeat.json (CPU and wall); optimization-sweep/results.json; original-failures.json; replay.hx/stdout; hxcpp-fresh-comparison.json. This was an audit: repository source files were not changed.","files":null}]}